WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.210
Darroch Greer, Filmmaker: The budget was in the end million dollars
and it was a million dollars Fred Rouse

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:11.010
our advisor we met with him early on and
he said "So you're talking about a

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.420
million dollar picture?" and I said "Oh no no no no I I think three hundred, three

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.619
hundred fifty thousand dollars."  And and I
remember think it'd be about two years

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:23.279
maybe two and a half years and but
because of the fundraising and because

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:27.300
the movie kept getting bigger it took us
seven years and in the end Fred was

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.009
exactly right and it was a million
dollars. Ron King, Filmmaker: Yeah, I mean I would think that

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:38.250
our film for the budget that we ended up
spending on it was probably a big

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:44.280
bargain because you know we did a lot of
the shoots with very very small crew

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.460
like one in some cases one person and
and then when we did get to New Zealand

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:55.680
we were flying with helicopters and
airplanes and all of that everybody who

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.320
was involved in that gave us lots of
discounts so you know I think it's more

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.409
of a two million dollar movie that got
made for a million dollars that's that's

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:08.070
why I like to think about it
but in the end what did that teach you

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.790
about raising money for a movie because
I know looking back on at seven years

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:16.409
prior do you think you actually would
have said yes to it knowing it wasn't

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:21.180
just gonna be a few years it wasn't just
gonna be three hundred thousand and all

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.390
that's gone on what do you think you
would have said yes I I've often

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:29.939
wondered about that because it would
seem very daunting but but the film grew

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:35.700
in such an organic way and and really we
never flagged I mean that you know there

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.210
was some times that were discouraging
and sometimes we're just out of money

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.920
and nothing was really happening but you
know having a partner passing the ball

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.430
back and forth and I was always
interested in the next thing we were

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:53.009
going to do I mean and we learned so
much I had I had done documentaries for

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:57.990
television but I had never done the
whole thing soup-to-nuts myself and from

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:03.149
the research which I really loved at the
writing to to actually shooting

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:08.039
airplanes and I went to Europe for five
weeks and shot there and went along the

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.950
Western Front and over the English
Channel and then

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:17.050
the movie just kept getting bigger and
bigger and more exciting and so there'd

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:22.270
be another hurdle and not enough we
always felt up for it

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:27.819
yeah I mean my sense throughout making
the movie was it was one of those ones

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:35.040
of sort of focus and also never taking
really never taking no for an answer

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.090
it's sort of like you have to be so
dedicated that you're you know people

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.690
are telling you no you can't do that
you're never gonna be able to do that

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:48.130
and you basically in your own mind and
in your own spirit

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:53.769
you basically say yes I can do that and
I'm going to do that you know I mean so

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:57.250
it's one of those things of believing in
the project and that you will accomplish

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:02.440
it and finish it that was always there
even when we were at the low points that

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.959
Derek mentioned there's still this thing
that's saying this movie is going to get

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:13.660
completed we are going to finish it
we're gonna follow through and I think

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.940
that if filmmakers keep that sort of
mindset as they work on their films it

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.989
it will get you get you to where you
want to go you just have to believe that

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:28.000
it's going to happen and keep following
that vision and it is persistence it

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.980
really is just showing up I mean there
were times when Ron and I would fly to

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:39.220
New York you know to meet some people to
go to a dinner party and come back with

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:45.640
nothing and but you know the cumulative
effect of being there and going on and

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:51.549
you know having a bit to show for the
effort you put in you know finally pays

00:03:51.549 --> 00:03:58.090
off and again as I as I said earlier the
film the film evolves you know and it

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:02.620
are often documentary filmmakers get
setbacks and they don't have the money

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.500
to edit or continue the film but that
time allows the story not only to grow

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:14.230
and maybe change but also to gestate and
then you come up with a much fuller and

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:20.109
richer film yeah yeah and the other
piece for young filmmakers or other

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:23.410
people starting out is I would just say
it's

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:28.720
start making the movie it doesn't matter
like if you have one picture and you're

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:35.350
making a documentary make a 30-second
video with that one picture with a

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:39.730
voiceover and maybe a little bit of
music behind it but demonstrate that you

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:43.870
can actually make something because
that's the thing that worked for us we

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.190
made little pieces of the movie over
time and we would show those pieces to

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:52.480
people and then people would finally say
oh oh you guys can actually make a movie

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:58.180
as opposed to just pitching an idea
because you don't have to have a lot of

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:03.490
resources to make a very simple piece
that will speak to your vision and what

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.810
you're trying to do you know so mate
start making the movie that's that's my

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:11.260
suggestion I completely agree with that
and there are two schools of thought

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:16.030
about that I've worked on other projects
where they said no we should we should

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:20.890
get all the money together first and you
know and then we won't be left hanging

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:26.110
and we'll we will know that we can do
this and and you know and I think you

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:29.380
have to prove yourself I mean I don't
know

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:33.910
Ken Burns doesn't have to or you know
very experienced filmmakers but you know

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:40.240
documentary films are curious individual
projects and they often don't work out

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.380
the way you've originally thought and so
yeah I think it's important to just

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:49.210
start working and sorry how far were you
into the process when you actually got

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:56.620
FedEx onboard as a sponsor FedEx the the
story with FedEx is that we were six

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:03.310
years in Wow we got an invitation to
screen the film at Ash Kosh which is the

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:09.850
largest air show in the United States
and we said yes to that even though we

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:15.990
didn't have a finished film and we
didn't have the finishing funds either

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:21.640
so this was this was one of those times
where we said yeah we'll be there we'll

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:26.440
have the movie we can screen it and then
we were like okay how the heck are we

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:32.110
gonna do this let's call Fred Smith
that's when finally our fundraising team

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:36.510
broke down and said yes let's and we had
been trying to reach out to fat

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:40.410
over time but finally it broke through
right at the moment that we needed them

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:46.080
and they came in at the very last minute
to save us basically so that we could

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.310
finish the film for oshkosh so again
showing up it sounds like you were

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:53.910
planning to go there too regardless yeah
even if you were going to show a little

00:06:53.910 --> 00:07:00.560
trailer we made the commitment but yeah
but they helped us out at the end

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.540
you

